Conditional Flow
Conditional flow also looks at options and makes decisions. Programs commonly use an if-then-
else statement to run different parts of the program based on these decisions. For example, in
a shoe store, a variable for the quantity of shoes in stock goes down as pairs are purchased.
When the counter hits zero, the if-then statement activates and adds to the daily report that
there are no more of a particular shoe in stock and it's time to order more. In pseudocode, it
would look like this: If quantity of shoes < 1, then add to the daily report, order more shoes.
Loops
Programs often run through loops, using FOR or WHILE, to repeat parts of the code as long
as certain conditions are met. The loop could run forever, or become an infinite loop, until a
variable meets a certain condition. For example, in a video game, the code continuously checks
the player's health. While health > 0, the game continues. But when health <= 0, the game is
over.
Calling Functions
A Function Call flow happens when a program gets a 'call' to go to another part of the program,
run that code, and then return to where it was before. When there is an error in a program, the
flow goes to the exception, or Exception Flow, to handle the issue.
Understanding and using program flow is important for writing good code. It also helps with
troubleshooting and debugging, and helps programmers understand the overall structure of the
program.
